  "question": {
    "text": "A metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, the stopping potential is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>, the stopping potential becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. The threshold wavelength for this metallic surface will be"
  },
